course: dictionary
lesson: 9141
title: Sneaky: sly, secret and clever
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Sneaky means doing things in a secret, sly way so that other people do not notice. «She felt sneaky all evening» tells you she had done something she was not proud of and was keeping it quiet.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Testing friends with a trick is a bit sneaky, though» shows that sneaky can mean a trick done to catch someone out. Ask yourself: would the meaning "secret and sly" fit here? If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Sneaky can also be small and everyday. «A sneaky biscuit» means a biscuit taken quietly when no one is looking. In maths, «a sneaky trick question» warns you that the obvious answer is the wrong one.
fact: Sneaky means done in a secret, sly way so others do not notice.
fact: Sneaky can also mean small, hidden and clever, like «a sneaky snack».
fact: A «sneaky trick question» warns that the obvious answer is wrong.
fact: The comparative forms are sneakier and sneakiest.
exercise: «He took a sneaky biscuit when no one was looking.» What does sneaky mean here? | *Done secretly, so no one notices | Very loud and noisy | Long and thin | Given away freely to everyone
exercise: A maths teacher says, «This is a sneaky trick question.» What does she warn you? | You must sing the answer | The answer is very big | The question is very easy | *The obvious answer is wrong
exercise: Which word could replace sneaky in «a sneaky look» without changing the meaning? | *secretive | loud | happy | tall
test: «She gave him a sneaky peek at her cards.» What does a sneaky peek mean? | Reading out loud to the class | Looking in a loud, open way | *Looking quietly, when she should not be seen | Closing her eyes tightly
test: Which best describes a sneaky thief? | *A sly person who takes things secretly | An honest person who gives things away | A baby who falls asleep | A teacher who shouts at the class
exam: Which is the best meaning of sneaky in «a sneaky little trick»? | Huge, loud and noisy | Kind, honest and open | *Small, hidden and clever | Slow, tired and sleepy
